 },
 "method": {
  "class": "Strategic targeted-investment analysis of multi-task leaderboards under aggregation rules: a candidate-implemented investment planner that, for a given public score matrix, aggregation rule, target model and investment model, returns the cheapest per-task score-gain plan that makes the target strictly first; plans are verified and costed by a trusted evaluator-owned rule engine and compared with evaluator-owned baselines and a reference solver.",
  "description": "Bounded claim. Investment model (declared before scoring, plane-independent): the developer raises the target model's score on a chosen set of tasks, gains >=0, independent per task, no cross-task spillover and no change to other models; two cost measures: L1 total points with per-task cap at the scale maximum (100), and L0 number of targeted tasks with each targeted task lifted at most to the best score in the field on that task. Field = the top-K (K=min(N,200)) fully-observed models by arithmetic mean; targets = the models at rank 2 and rank 10 under the arithmetic mean; success = target strictly first (ties count against the target) under the rule recomputed by the evaluator. Rules: mean, median, trimmed_mean_20 (drop lowest and highest 20% of tasks), zscore_mean (per-task standardized, recomputed on the modified matrix), borda (mean per-task rank), plurality (number of tasks on which a model is strictly first), copeland (pairwise task-majority wins), maximin (worst-task score). On the pinned public snapshots (HELM MMLU v1.13.0 for search; Open LLM Leaderboard contents and results snapshots for final) the claim is conditional: (i) the minimal investment found by the evaluator-owned reference solver is expected to vary with the aggregation rule (hypothesis, to be supported, refuted or qualified by the paired per-case reference costs, including infeasible cases) and with how correlated task scores are, tested against a within-task shuffled-score null; (ii) a candidate planner is credited only for plans that the trusted verifier confirms, scored relative to the reference solver, and is claimed better than the evaluator-owned naive baselines only if its paired final-plane main efficiency is strictly higher. No claim is made about real training effects, other leaderboards, other fields sizes, spillover or heterogeneous task costs; sensitivity to K, cap policy and field is reported as a limitation or an explicit robustness check, not assumed."
 },
